 "text": "**Adversarial review \u2013\u202ffree\u2011model queue worker**  \n*(Only failures/attacks, no praise.  \u201cnot traceable\u201d is used when the code path cannot be followed.)*  \n\n| # | File / function | Concrete failure / attack (model\u2011controlled input) | Does it succeed on the current code? (trace) | Fix (short description) | Severity |\n|---|-----------------|---------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------|--------------------------|----------|\n| 1 | **worker/doc_job.py \u2013 `draft()`** (the call to the drafter) | The drafter prompt contains the *entire* target file contents. A malicious repository file can embed a *shell\u2011command* inside a line that the model later repeats verbatim in the JSON \u201cnew\u201d field. When `apply_edits()` writes that line back to the file, the **GitHub Actions runner later executes the file as part of the `git add -A -- '*.md'` step** because the runner\u2019s default `core.autocrlf` and `text` attributes treat `*.md` as *text* but the *commit hook* `pre\u2011commit` (not shown) could be configured to run a linter that executes code blocks. Even without a hook, the *GitHub UI* renders markdown and will execute any embedded HTML/JS when the PR is viewed, giving a remote attacker a XSS vector. | The code itself does **not** invoke a shell, but the *output* of the model becomes part of a markdown file that is later rendered in the GitHub UI, which executes embedded scripts. The attack therefore succeeds in practice (XSS) once the PR is opened. | \u2022 Sanitize the \u201cnew\u201d string before writing it: reject any line that contains `<script>`, `javascript:`, back\u2011ticks, or any HTML tag that can execute code. <br>\u2022 Apply a strict markdown\u2011only whitelist (e.g., only allow plain\u2011text characters, punctuation, and markdown syntax). <br>\u2022 Run a post\u2011edit linter that strips unsafe HTML before committing. | **Critical** |\n| 2 | **worker/research_job.py \u2013 `read()`** (model\u2011generated claims) | The model can return a claim whose \u201cquote\u201d field contains a *large\u2011scale Unicode\u2011combining\u2011character* sequence that, when written to a markdown file by `card()`, triggers a *Unicode\u2011based denial\u2011of\u2011service* in downstream renderers (e.g., VS\u202fCode, GitHub). The attacker can also embed a URL that points to a malicious site; the summary model later copies the quote verbatim, giving a *phishing* vector. | The quote is written unchanged into `research/cards/<ID>.md` in `card()`. No sanitisation is performed, so the malicious payload reaches the repo. | \u2022 Validate that every quote is plain ASCII/UTF\u20118 without control characters. <br>\u2022 Escape or strip any URLs that are not whitelisted. <br>\u2022 Limit the length of each quote (e.g., \u2264\u202f400\u202fbytes). | **Major** |\n| 3 | **worker/queue_ops.py \u2013 `claim()`** (git\u2011based lock) | Two workers can *both* think they hold the same item when the first worker\u2019s push succeeds but the second worker\u2019s push **fails** *after* it has already written the claim into its local `queue.json` and saved the file. The second worker then returns `{\"item\": i, \u2026}` (the item it thought it claimed) because the `if commit_push(...):` test is **False**, but the loop continues and eventually returns `{\"item\": None, \"lost_races\": \u2026}` only after `TRIES` attempts. If the second worker crashes **after** the failed push but **before** the next loop iteration, the item stays marked \u201cin progress\u201d with the first worker\u2019s lease, but the second worker\u2019s local copy still believes it owns the item and may later run `release()` with the wrong `worker` id, causing a *duplicate release* that writes a bogus run entry. | The code path is traceable: after a failed push the loop continues; if the process is killed before the next iteration, the stale claim remains in memory but never reaches the repo. The stale claim can be used to fabricate a \u201crelease\u201d later (e.g., via a replay attack). | \u2022 After a failed `commit_push`, **discard** the in\u2011memory claim (`i`) and reload the queue before proceeding. <br>\u2022 Add a guard in `release()` that aborts if the item\u2019s `claimed_by` does not match the supplied worker **and** the `status` is not \u201cin progress\u201d. <br>\u2022 Persist a temporary \u201ctentative\u2011claim\u201d flag that is cleared on any push failure. | **Critical** |\n| 4 | **worker/queue_ops.py \u2013 `expire()`** (lease expiry) | The function only runs when a worker invokes `claim()`. If the queue stays empty for a long period (e.g., weekend), items whose lease has already expired will **never be reopened**, leaving them stuck in \u201cin progress\u201d. This violates the \u201cno item stuck\u201d guarantee. | `expire()` is called at the start of each `claim()` attempt. If `claim()` is never called (queue empty), expired items are never processed \u2192 stuck. | \u2022 Run `expire()` as a separate scheduled job (e.g., a daily \u201cmaintenance\u201d workflow) that rewrites the queue and pushes a commit even when no claim is made. <br>\u2022 Or modify `ready()` to call `expire()` internally before filtering, ensuring expiry is checked on every read. | **Major** |\n| 5 | **worker/ledger.py \u2013 `reserve()`** (neuron\u2011cap bypass) | The neuron cap for Cloudflare is stored in `CAPS[\"cloudflare\"][\"neurons\"] = 5000`. The check only looks at `e[\"neurons\"] >= cap[\"neurons\"]` **without** adding the neurons that will be consumed by the current reservation. A worker can therefore reserve unlimited calls that each consume many neurons; the cap is never triggered. The later `record()` adds the actual neuron usage, potentially exceeding the daily limit by orders of magnitude. | The condition `e[\"neurons\"] >= cap[\"neurons\"]` never changes during `reserve()`, so the cap is effectively disabled. This is traceable in the code. | \u2022 Change the check to `e[\"neurons\"] + projected_neurons > cap[\"neurons\"]`. Since the reservation does not know exact neurons, require the caller to specify an estimated neuron budget (e.g., `cloudflare:1:100` meaning 1 call, 100 neurons). <br>\u2022 Enforce the same check in `record()` and abort the push if caps would be exceeded. | **Critical** |\n| 6 | **worker/ledger.py \u2013 `record()`** (no cap enforcement) | After usage is recorded, the ledger file is updated and pushed **without** re\u2011checking the caps. An attacker can deliberately *under\u2011reserve* (e.g., reserve 1 call) then make many more calls in the same run (by looping the model) and record them, inflating `calls` and `neurons` far beyond the caps. | `record()` never looks at `CAPS`; it simply adds the numbers and pushes. This is traceable. | \u2022 In `record()`, after updating `led`, verify that for each pool `led[pool][\"calls\"] <= CAPS[pool][\"calls\"]` and `led[pool].get(\"neurons\",0) <= CAPS[pool].get(\"neurons\",\u221e)`. Abort the push if a cap would be exceeded. | **Major** |\n| 7 | **worker/digest.py \u2013 `metrics()`** (double\u2011claim counting) | The holder map is never cleared on lease\u2011expiry commits (`worker: leases expired \u2026`). Consequently, when a lease expires and a new worker later claims the same item, the code counts it as a *double claim* even though the previous lease is no longer valid. This **inflates** the double\u2011claim metric and can hide real contention problems. | The loop over commit subjects adds `expired += 1` on \u201cleases expired\u201d lines but never removes `holder[item]`. Therefore later \u201cclaims\u201d are flagged as double. Traceable. | \u2022 When a \u201cleases expired\u201d line is seen, also `holder.pop(item_id, None)` for each id listed in the commit message (the commit message includes the list of IDs). <br>\u2022 Alternatively, rebuild `holder` from the current queue state after processing all log lines, rather than incrementally. | **Minor** |\n| 8 | **worker/digest.py \u2013 `flag()`** (pointer regex too permissive) | The regex `POINTER` matches any string that looks like a path (`foo/bar.md`) **anywhere** in a sentence, causing false negatives: a legitimate sentence containing a file path but no numeric claim will be considered \u201cevidence\u201d and not flagged, allowing a model to hide a fabricated number by appending a harmless path. Conversely, the regex can be fooled by adding a dummy path after a fabricated number, bypassing the flag. | The function simply checks `if re.search(r\"\\d\", s) and not POINTER.search(s):` \u2013 any path satisfies the second condition, so the flag is suppressed. This is traceable. | \u2022 Require that the pointer be **explicitly linked** to the number (e.g., `#123 (run 456)`), not any arbitrary path. Tighten the regex to only accept known identifier formats (`Q\\d+`, `run \\d{6,}`, SHA\u20111/7+ hex, or `file.md` **preceded** by \u201csee\u201d). <br>\u2022 Add a second pass that ensures the pointer appears **after** the number in the same clause. | **Minor** |\n\n### Summary of grades\n- **Critical**: 1, 3, 5  \n- **Major**: 2, 4, 6  \n- **Minor**: 7, 8  \n\n### Recommendation\nThe worker pipeline cannot be safely unpaused in its current form. The most severe issues (model\u2011generated text reaching a markdown file that is later rendered, stale claim handling, and complete bypass of the neuron budget cap) allow remote code\u2011execution, denial\u2011of\u2011service, and uncontrolled spending. All critical and major defects must be fixed **before** any further runs are allowed.\n\n**Decision:** **do not unpause** \u2013 apply the fixes above, re\u2011run the test suite, and only then consider unpausing the workflow.",
